2.8.11-ifsnop.1 (python_v0.7.11 unchanged)
First version of the fork https://github.com/ifsnop/asterix, based on CroatiaControlLtd/asterix 2.8.11.
The original project has not been updated since December 2025.
Security fixes (found with AddressSanitizer/UBSan and fuzzing):
           Out-of-bounds reads when computing item lengths of truncated or malformed records
           Out-of-bounds reads in PCAP link/IP/UDP headers and ORADIS headers, endless loop with ORADIS byte count 0
           Stack overflow parsing BDS entries of -LF filter files
           Memory leak with ORADIS input
           Invalid casts of Repetitive sub-formats (BDS) to Fixed
           Non printable characters in ASCII fields copied to the output
           Stack and static buffer overflows building file names in the disk device
           TCP client socket used after close, definition object leaked when asterix.ini is missing
Source IP of each record in TXT, JSON and XML outputs (UDP multicast input and GPS files)
New output format -jx / --json-hex: compact JSON, one record per line, with hex and mask of each field

2.8.11-ifsnop.2 (python_v0.7.11 unchanged)
-jx / --json-hex made to change a value and write it back to ASTERIX:
           "fspec" (FSPEC bytes) and "hexdata" (data items after the FSPEC): the record is fspec+hexdata
           "pos" in every field: byte offset of its hex in hexdata (first byte of hexdata is pos 0)
           "mask" whenever a field does not fill its hex bytes, so hexdata[pos..] = (hex & ~mask) | (value & mask)
           Unique keys: FX bits numbered by extent (FX0, FX1, ...), other repeated names numbered (spare, spare1)
-j record header changed (incompatible with the original): "fspec" printed separately and "hexdata" holds
           only the data items after the FSPEC (in the original, hexdata was the whole record)

2.8.11-ifsnop.3 (python_v0.7.11 unchanged)
Faster decoding with byte-identical output: -jx about 8x faster (51 MB GPS file: 68 s -> 8.5 s),
           other output formats 2-2.6x faster:
           format() formats once into a stack buffer instead of twice through the heap
           -jx fields written without allocations (hex, mask and pos built in place, mask built once per field)
           Items written straight into the output string instead of copied through temporary strings
           -jx unique keys computed once per format instead of a std::set per field
           Cached FRN -> item and Compound presence-bit lookups
           Disk input: no ftell/fstat/select system calls per packet
make rebuilds objects when an included header changes (dependency targets were never matched);
           run make clean once after updating

2.8.11-ifsnop.4 (python_v0.7.11 unchanged)
Record header with the FSPEC apart in -jh, -je and the default text output, as in -j and -jx (incompatible):
           "fspec" (JSON) / "Fspec:" (text) has the FSPEC bytes and "hexdata" / "HexData:" only the data
           items after the FSPEC: the record is fspec+hexdata. XML keeps the whole record in hexdata.
-h documents the default text output and lists -G and -xh in the usage line
Format strings: text from input data or file names is never used as a printf format
           (a definitions file name with % made the program crash); format/argument type mismatches fixed
Python publish workflow runs only by hand; CodeQL workflow updated (codeql-action v4, make build)
Text fields (ASCII encoding: callsign, aircraft type, airports, runway, stand, SID/STAR, CAT004
           designators...) only print A-Z, 0-9, space and '.' (radio channel names, I205/090); any other
           byte is printed as '@'. A quote or backslash in damaged data made -j/-jx invalid JSON.
           Visible on captures that pad these fields with control characters: they used to print as
           spaces and now print as '@' (e.g. callsign DLHEGN + 0x01 -> DLHEGN@). -jx "hex" keeps the raw bytes.
Malformed records: decoding goes on after them as before, and a WARNING on stderr gives the record
           number and the whole data block in hex (CAT, LEN, data) for later analysis. Decoder messages
           are no longer cut at 1024 characters.
Fixes found by the new test suite:
           HDLC input from files: files under 4 KB decoded nothing and longer files lost their last
           partial 4 KB buffer
           HDLC: a frame with an Asterix block length of 0 looped forever
           PCAP: a corrupt packet length (incl_len) allocated up to 4 GB and could abort (std::bad_alloc);
           lengths over 262144 bytes or past the end of the file are rejected
           FINAL: a byte count smaller than the record header wrapped around to almost 4 GB (same abort)
           XMLParser: a definitions file that failed inside <Category> leaked that category
Test suite (tests/, see tests/README.md), run by GitHub Actions on every push and pull request:
           golden outputs: 13 inputs x 8 output formats x with and without -LF, including synthetic FINAL,
           HDLC and ORADIS files generated from the real samples (tests/tools/gen_samples.py)
           -jx consistency (fspec, length, crc, hex/mask/pos/val, unique keys) and agreement of -j, -jh,
           -je, text and XML; write-back of edited values through hex/mask/pos
           stdin, UDP multicast and loop mode input; command line options
           malformed inputs (one per fixed bug) and mutated inputs under AddressSanitizer/UBSan, valid JSON
           whatever the input; unit tests (bit fields, getLength contract of every item of every category,
           parser, UAP selection, record headers); libFuzzer; line coverage floor (65%, from 48%)
CMake: minimum version 3.10; the decoder is a static library; options ASTERIX_TESTS, ASTERIX_FUZZ and
           ASTERIX_COVERAGE. install/test/test.sh prints the diff of a failed case.

2.8.11-ifsnop.5 (python_v0.7.11 unchanged)
-j, -jh, -x and -xh about twice as fast as in ifsnop.4, output byte for byte the same: the field key,
           numeric value and text value no longer go through format() (51 MB GPS file: -j 15.4 s -> 7.7 s,
           39.9 s before any optimization; -jh 15.6 -> 8.5 s; -x 17.6 -> 8.8 s; -xh 22.0 -> 10.8 s)
README: build and coverage badges of this fork; the coverage job publishes the line coverage to the
           badges branch on each push to master

2.8.11-ifsnop.6 (python_v0.7.11 unchanged)
-LF about 40% faster: items with no field in the filter are only measured to step over them, never
           copied, walked or printed; the -j/-jx record header no longer goes through format()
           (2.7 GB GPS capture, "bzip2 -dc | asterix -LF ... -G -j": 5m05s -> 2m59s)
stdin is read in 64 KB blocks instead of 3 read() + 1 select() per packet; live input is not delayed
-LF fix: an item not in the filter holding an empty Repetitive (e.g. CAT062 I390 "TOD":[]) was printed;
           it is now left out. With -LF, errors inside items not in the filter are no longer reported
